{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`rapt-dreadable-dromedary` (`aefe-614a-2631-4117`) put\n`rebar._store.store_usability.store_is_usable` on the library/MCP read chokepoint\n(`_reads._tracker`). The predicate spawns `git -C <tracker> rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` on\nEVERY read.\n\nThe approved plan reasoned this was negligible because \"the read path ALREADY spawns git\n(throttled `git fetch`/reconverge via `_fresh`)\". That reasoning is not quite right: `_fresh`\nis throttled to at most once per minute, so on the great majority of reads there is NO git\nspawn today. The predicate adds an UNTHROTTLED one.\n\n## Measurement (this worktree, off `origin/main` 565e1856)\n\nA 25-ticket store, `REBAR_NO_SYNC=1`, 40 iterations, predicate neutralized by monkeypatching\n`store_usability.store_is_usable` to a constant (so only the probe is removed, not the guard):\n\n```\nlist_tickets WITH predicate:    22.7 ms\nlist_tickets WITHOUT predicate:  5.6 ms\npredicate overhead:             17.1 ms  (305%)\n```\n\nSo on a small store the probe costs roughly 4x the read itself. In absolute terms it is one\ngit process startup (~17ms on this host). On the production tracker, log reduction dominates\nand the relative cost is small; the 4x is a relative figure on an already-fast operation.\n\n## Why it was not addressed in the parent change\n\nThe parent change shipped the operator-approved, plan-review-certified design unchanged. The\ntwo obvious optimizations both have real downsides that need a deliberate decision rather than\nan in-flight one:\n\n- **Caching the verdict.** A cached \"usable\" answer is precisely the staleness hazard the\n  parent bug is about: a store that becomes unusable mid-session would keep reading as usable.\n  Any cache needs an explicit invalidation story.\n- **Resolving HEAD in-process.** Reading `.git/HEAD` and following the ref by hand\n  reimplements git ref resolution (packed-refs, `.git`-as-a-file for worktrees/submodules,\n  detached HEAD), which is fragile — and the parent change deliberately chose the\n  `footprint._git` prior-art pattern over that.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] A decision is recorded (in this ticket) on whether the ~17ms per-read probe is accepted\n      as-is, or is to be reduced, and by which mechanism.\n- [x] If reduced: the same measurement harness shows the overhead drop, AND the regression\n      tests in `tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py` still pass unchanged — the\n      broken/no-`.git`/mid-clone cases must still raise `store_uninitialized`.\n- [x] If reduced by caching: a test proves a store that becomes unusable DURING a session is\n      detected, not served from a stale \"usable\" verdict.\n- [x] If accepted as-is: the reasoning is recorded on `store_usability.py` so the next reader\n      does not re-litigate it.\n\n## Decision / Implementation Plan\n\nChoose the reduce-by-durable-structure mechanism, not caching. Best-practice precedent is to\navoid caching a filesystem health predicate whose whole job is to notice state changes, and to\nprefer a cheap durable sentinel already committed in the store before spawning a subprocess. In\nthis repo that sentinel is `.store-compat.json`, with the legacy event-directory fallback already\naccepted by `store_is_usable` for gitless materialized snapshots.\n\nImplementation: reorder `store_is_usable` so it first accepts a tracker that carries rebar store\nstructure, and only falls back to the bounded `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` probe when no store\nstructure is present. That keeps the guard live on every read with no stale cache: a marker-only\nbroken store still has neither structure nor resolvable HEAD and raises `store_uninitialized`; a\nmid-clone store with `.git` but no checked-out structure still raises until HEAD or structure\nexists; a materialized gitless snapshot remains readable. No new mechanism is introduced.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/store_usability.py` — reorder the existing structure and HEAD checks and record\n  why the structure-first path removes the per-read git spawn without caching.\n- `tests/unit/test_reads_uninitialized_store.py` — add the RED regression proving initialized stores\n  carrying committed structure do not spawn git on each usability check, while existing broken,\n  gitless snapshot, and mid-clone tests remain unchanged.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788837293581441001,"uuid":"5960db22-f432-4e2e-ae00-0445ece871f9"}